WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.330
The worldview three
satellite passing over

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.910
southern Japan spies
something in the forest

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:05.540
below.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.180
This report shows that
Japanese scientists were

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.330
running an experiment to
see how a space affects

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.690
tree growth. So what
they did was plant ten

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.610
concentric rings of
trees.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.210
They had the same amount
of trees in each ring.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.550
So when you were closer
to the center, of course

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.990
they were closer to each
other.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.460
In a feat of superhuman
scientific endurance.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.790
Over the next half
century, botanists

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.520
painstakingly monitor
how each ring of trees

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.330
develops.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.080
And as you get out from
that center, they get

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.800
more spaced, more room
for sunlight and more

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.530
room for their roots to
grow and get water.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.940
So of course, they grew
taller.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.230
While the scientific
importance of this

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.800
strange experiment may
be debatable, its impact

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.360
from space is hard to
deny.

